  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1158 PM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\n\nFor Wednesday, it will be a few degrees cooler over the region, \nhowever we will still have some weak favorable lift over the region \nas the jet will still remain just ot our west and south. Likewise \nwill still see the best chance of showers and storms as today, \nhowever we will see a bit better chance of some showers and storms \nover the plains. \n\nThursday through Saturday, Temperature will be similar all 3\ndays, with Upper 70s to mid 80s over the plains and 65 to 70 \nover the mtns, Isolated to scattered showers will continue over\nthe mtns during this time period as moisture will linger over \nthe higher terrain. \n\nSunday things are likely going to begin to occur as a strong Pacific \ntrough pushes in from the northwest and begins to develop a \nsignificant trough over the western one third of the CONUS. \nBoth the deterministic GFS and EC show this trough, but \ndifferences in moisture/orientation/track and timing are noted \nbetween the guidance, and this variability is shown in the \nstatistical products. Overall confidence is high that it will \nget much cooler/(colder) early-mid next week and the mountains \nwill likely see precipitation. \n\nOverall, how impactful this system will be dependent on how much \ntropical/subtropical moisture aloft will be advected into the \nregion, and this will depend how much moisture from tropical \ncyclones off the mexican coast will be drawn into the mid level \ntrough. \n\nThere is still quite a bit of uncertainty with this system, but \nthere is potential this could be a higher impactful event\naffecting our region next week. \\/Hodanish",
